86553471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553471 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553471 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553471 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553471 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d0d6b55c-5888-5098-8079-b16eed82738c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553474 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7838ff7-32b3-503c-9d51-a06dd6fe14d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553498 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+4}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "137d2706-a3fe-517c-a8b7-db4dd05fae28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553498 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-2)=sY(s)+2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(t)=sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{sin\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s+4}+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553509 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)\\,,\\,y(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{5}{s-3}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=5{e}^{3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "305f1606-0562-59f2-b1e4-3359528e4a32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553509 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s-3)+B(s-3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}-\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s-3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}-\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s-3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)(-\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{t-2}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}) \\\\  & =u(t-2)(-\\frac{3t-5}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=5{e}^{3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{3t-5}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553543 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "847985b8-e342-54d5-a829-10e82cf593d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553543 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2=A((-1)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,5A=2\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n2=4A+C\\,⟹\\,C=2-\\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{-s+1}{{s}^{2}+4})=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{2}{5}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\