dot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $f(t)=cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /3)s}\\cdot L\\{cos\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553582 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e21a8112-0441-5b7a-9e9e-a86738fe8b02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553582 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-2y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=4{e}^{t-3}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{4u(t-3)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{4{e}^{(t+3)-3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{4{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{4}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} \\\\ sY(s)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
